 S~S TruSoft Computing LLC (TSC LLC) Repository Philosophy

------------------------------------------

 S~S  The Philosophy is stated somewhere else.

   The SimpleSal repository README explains how a SimpleSal App, such as this one, is constructed.

   There is additional information available on the "substack.com" site for user "SimpleSal".

------------------------------------------

=====================

  S~S The Elevator Pitch

------------------------------------------

    The processor in an embedded gadget powers up and runs software that exists in Time and Space.

    When you create a space where virtual objects exist and act over Time, you need a Time Keeper.

    When the software you write and put into a gadget exists over Time, events occur over Time.

    A SimpleSal App co-exists with ssTEA, software that is a Time Keeper and an Event Manager.

    This basic App reports "the current Time" maintained by ssTEA, over Time, using Time/Data Events.

------------------------------------------

=====================

 S~S This Repository

------------------------------------------

 S~S  What is the purpose of the objects in this repository or repo?

    * The repo contains an example of the simplest or most basic SimpleSal App: display the Time.
      The difference with the app-time basic SimpleSal App is that Time and Data Events are used.

      * Even the simplest of Apps may be configurable at build time.  This App will either build to
        set "the Current Time" as: zero, or, the Time at which the software was built (BuiltAt).

            In the former, the numbers displayed appear to be a count-up timer, from zero.
            In the latter, the numbers displayed appear to be a strange clock, showing the Time.

        The alternative would require two repos, each with an App identical except in name and
        a few lines of source code difference in the App: Time initialization to zero or BuiltAt.

    * Quick explanation of the value being displayed by the App:

        [flags|days.hours.minutes.seconds.milliseconds.microseconds.nanoseconds.plancks]

        plancks and nanoseconds are not detectable, microseconds accuracy is platform-specific.

------------------------------------------

    * The purpose of displaying the Time or Timer is to allow an external comparison between what
      the processor running at a frequency is calculating and what is known or trusted to be right.

      When a processor is executing a signal algorithm, knowledge of Time is fundamental.  When
      the processor slows down due to heat, the algorithm within the processor cannot perceive it.

      This is what complex multi-processor chip software does to detect one processor overheating;
      when the processor heats up, it slows down, and its calculation of Time begins to slow also.

      Another processor, known to never overload, perceives the slow down and reduces frequencies.
      The reduction in the frequency will reduce the amount of heat generated by the frequency.

      And this is just the first SimpleSal App, on the road to writing signal algorithm software!

------------------------------------------

 S~S  What are the contents of this repository?

   * The managed object is everything, and only that, in the rr\app-events\ directory

       * this App works with any version of SimpleSal, therefore there is no rr\app-events\SimpleSal\

       * rr\app-events\app-events.ino

       * rr\app-events\*.h

         the App depends on SimpleSal; the "mesa*.h" and "ss_configuration.h" file are derived from
         the version of SimpleSal named in the release note (see SimpleSal/simplesal README).

       * because this App does initiate Time and Data Events, the ssTEA helper for the API ia used.

    * TSC LLC promises industry-industry standard backwards compatibility: old stuff always works.

------------------------------------------

 S~S  What happens in my workspace as a result of interacting with this repository?

    The managed object will be placed into your workspace, along with the human metadata files
    (depending on your ability to configure the repo App to only consider files matching filters).

    The only reason to have a metadata copy locally is if files are being modified for a release.

------------------------------------------

 S~S  What about the version of the SimpleSal software the App co-exists with?

    SimpleSal promises backwards compatibility with older versions; basic features do not disappear.

        This means users should be able to update to new versions, as needed, without side-effects.

    Visit the username SimpleSal's repo named /simplesal, clone the latest "released" version tag.

